<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CustomField xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS COMMENT SITS INSIDE THE ROOT ELEMENT DELIBERATELY — see
        Protection_Window_Days__c.field-meta.xml in this folder for why a comment above the
        root breaks `sf` deploy at conversion.

        WHAT THIS STORES. Measured, not guessed. RE-MEASURED 2026-08-30 (WS8 review, W2):
        this said 596 + 18,658 = 19,254, which was the PRE-WS7 prompt; WS7 added 1,338
        characters to ENRICHED_EXTRACTION_RULES the same day. Concatenating
        LLMExtractionCalloutService.LEGACY_EXTRACTION_RULES (596 chars) and
        ENRICHED_EXTRACTION_RULES (19,996 chars) — i.e. the current
        EXTRACTION_INSTRUCTION constant — is 20,592 characters, matching
        shippedPrompt()'s documented length and the loader's own seed-length check.
        length=131072 (the
        LongTextArea ceiling) leaves ~6.8x headroom over the measured size, which this
        prompt has already grown into repeatedly per its own class-header revision log
        (LLMExtractionCalloutService.cls's header documents at least 8 dated prompt
        revisions). LEGACY_RESPONSE_FORMAT (166 chars) is DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED — see below.

        WHY LEGACY_RESPONSE_FORMAT STAYS IN APEX (P4, design-requirements.md §1). Its own
        Javadoc (LLMExtractionCalloutService.cls:500-503): "kept ONLY as the rollback lever
        described in the class header. Nothing references it on the enriched path." Putting
        a rollback lever in editable Setup config defeats the lever — if the enriched prompt
        ever needs rolling back, that rollback must not itself depend on Setup data staying
        untouched. This was a scope decision made after the design doc, not an oversight.

        THREE NEW FAILURE MODES THIS FIELD CREATES (design-requirements.md §2/WS5,
        restated here because this is where an editor will actually be looking):
          1. The prompt text hardcodes "at most " + MAX_PROPERTIES_IN_PROMPT (10,
             LLMExtractionCalloutService) as English prose. MAX_PROPERTIES_IN_PROMPT stays
             an Apex constant (out of scope per this build's brief). Editing the prose
             without also changing that constant lets the stated limit and the enforced
             limit silently diverge.
          2. The prompt text hardcodes the restricted picklist value lists for
             asset_type, deal_type, sale_process and listing_status. LLMExtractionParser
             describe-validates the model's answer against the LIVE picklist — a prompt
             value that is not an active picklist/record-type value makes the parser drop
             it SILENTLY (no exception, no log), and the resulting Lead/Opportunity gets no
             deal type and therefore no record type. See LLMExtractionCalloutService.cls
             lines 765-772 for the D3/D4 migration incident this already caused once.
          3. Editing ANY part of this prompt re-keys the broker registry:
             broker_email and property_address are Property_Registry__c's claim key, and
             this text is what determines how the model extracts both. There is NO
             automated regression guard on this text (ExtractionRegressionFixtureTest never
             pinned ENRICHED_EXTRACTION_RULES — see P3, design-requirements.md §1 — and its
             LEGACY_EXTRACTION_RULES / LEGACY_RESPONSE_FORMAT pins were removed as part of
             this same workstream). The only mitigation available: re-run the two manual
             fixtures LLMExtractionCalloutService's header names ("RE-RUN BOTH AFTER ANY
             MODEL-VERSION CHANGE and after any edit to this block") before and after any
             edit here, and diff the normalised addresses by hand.

        RECORD LOADING. This field's value is loaded by
        scripts/load-broker-protection-config.apex (not built by this task — see the parent
        object file's comment and this build's handoff for the exact string the loader must
        write and why an Apex string-literal complexity limit forces it to be built by
        chunked concatenation, not one big literal).
    -->
    <fullName>Extraction_Prompt__c</fullName>
    <description>The full extraction instruction sent to the model on every callout (LEGACY_EXTRACTION_RULES + ENRICHED_EXTRACTION_RULES concatenated; ~19,250 characters as of 2026-08-30). 🔴 EDITING THIS RE-KEYS THE BROKER REGISTRY: broker_email and property_address are the Property_Registry__c claim key, so changing these instructions changes claim outcomes with no deploy and no review. 🔴 Hardcodes MAX_PROPERTIES_IN_PROMPT (10) and the restricted picklist values for deal_type / sale_process / listing_status — drift from the matching Apex constant or picklist silently loses properties or drops the deal type, with no exception and no log. Run the two manual regression fixtures named in LLMExtractionCalloutService&apos;s header before and after any edit, and diff the normalised addresses by hand. EXCLUDES LEGACY_RESPONSE_FORMAT (stays hardcoded, a deliberately inert rollback lever). See the in-root XML comment for full detail.</description>
    <fieldManageability>DeveloperControlled</fieldManageability>
    <inlineHelpText>🔴 Re-keys the broker registry claim key when edited. Re-run the two manual regression fixtures before and after any change. See description.</inlineHelpText>
    <label>Extraction Prompt</label>
    <length>131072</length>
    <required>false</required>
    <type>LongTextArea</type>
    <visibleLines>20</visibleLines>
</CustomField>
